course: religion-ethics
lesson: 102
title: Olympiad: Bible stories in order and festivals of many faiths ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Olympiad puzzles about faith rarely ask for one fact; they ask you to put stories in order, link a festival to the story it remembers, count days on a calendar, or spot the one claim that does not fit.
explain: The Bible's big story has a family line you can use as a ladder: Abraham, then his son Isaac, then Isaac's son Jacob, then Jacob's son Joseph, who brought the family to Egypt; long after, Moses led their descendants out, and the Ten Commandments came on the way.
explain: Festivals are clues too: Jews remember the Exodus at Passover, Christians remember the Last Supper, which the Gospels place at Passover time, and Easter follows Good Friday; Pentecost comes fifty days after Easter, counting Easter itself as day one.
explain: Different faiths count time differently: the Jewish Shabbat runs from Friday sunset to Saturday sunset, Muslims gather for Friday midday prayer, most Christians worship on Sunday, and the Islamic months follow the moon, so Ramadan comes about eleven days earlier each year.
explain: When you speak about any faith, say what its followers believe: "Christians believe Jesus rose from the dead", "Muslims believe the Qur'an is God's word"; a fair answer reports a belief truly without mocking it and without pretending everyone shares it.
fact: In the Bible, Abraham's son was Isaac, Isaac's son was Jacob, and Jacob's twelve sons included Joseph, whose brothers sold him and who later brought the whole family to Egypt.
fact: Moses lived long after Joseph; he led the Israelites out of Egypt (the Exodus), and on the journey God gave the Ten Commandments at Mount Sinai.
fact: Ruth was the great-grandmother of King David (Ruth 4:17); David fought Goliath as a young man, later became king, and his son Solomon built the Temple in Jerusalem.
fact: Hanukkah remembers the Temple in Jerusalem being made holy again, many centuries after Solomon; on each of its eight nights one more candle is lit, using a helper candle called the shamash.
fact: John's Gospel says Jesus was in the Temple at the Feast of Dedication, which is Hanukkah (John 10:22-23).
fact: Passover remembers the Exodus; the Gospels set the Last Supper at Passover time, and Christians remember it on Maundy Thursday, the day before Good Friday.
fact: Christians believe Jesus rose on the third day (Easter Day), went up to heaven forty days later (Ascension), and that the Holy Spirit came at Pentecost, fifty days after Easter counting Easter as the first day.
fact: Paul became a follower of Jesus on the road to Damascus after Pentecost, and wrote letters to the young churches.
fact: The church year runs Advent, Christmas, Epiphany, Shrove Tuesday, Ash Wednesday and Lent, Holy Week, Easter, Pentecost; Easter's date moves because it is fixed by the moon.
fact: Lent is forty days not counting Sundays, so from Ash Wednesday to Easter Day is longer than forty days on the calendar.
fact: The Islamic calendar follows the moon and is about eleven days shorter than the sun's year, so Ramadan and Eid al-Fitr move earlier each year and can fall in any season.
fact: Muslims believe Isa (Jesus) was a great prophet born of Mary but not the Son of God; Christians believe he is the Son of God.
fact: Diwali, the autumn festival of lights, is kept by Hindus, Sikhs and Jains; Hanukkah is the Jewish festival of lights; Eid al-Fitr ends Ramadan; Wesak (Vesak) is when many Buddhists remember the Buddha's birth, enlightenment and death.
fact: Slang check: "innit" means isn't it; "lowkey" means a bit or secretly; "fixin' to" means about to; "a mo" means a moment.
exercise: Put these in Bible order: Moses at Mount Sinai, Joseph sold by his brothers, Abraham leaves home, David fights Goliath. Which comes third? | Moses at Mount Sinai | Joseph sold by his brothers | David fights Goliath | Abraham leaves home
exercise: Jake says, "Joseph's great-grandad was Abraham." Is he right? | Yes: Abraham, then Isaac, then Jacob, then Joseph | No, Abraham was Joseph's dad | No, Joseph was Abraham's grandad | No, they lived in different books and never connected
exercise: Which of these happened LAST? | Solomon builds the Temple | Ruth marries Boaz | David fights Goliath | The Israelites cross out of Egypt
exercise: Tilly says, "The Ten Commandments were given before Joseph went to Egypt." What is wrong? | They came after the Exodus, which was long after Joseph | Nothing, she is right about the order | The commandments were given in Babylon to Daniel | Joseph himself carried the tablets down from the mountain
exercise: A riddle: "I came from Moab, I told my mother-in-law 'Where you go, I will go', and my great-grandson was King David. Who am I?" | Ruth | Sarah | Goliath | Rahab
exercise: Which festival remembers something that happened in the Temple in Jerusalem long after both Moses and Solomon? | Hanukkah | Passover | Pentecost | Christmas
exercise: On each night of Hanukkah one more candle is lit: 1, then 2, up to 8. Not counting the helper candle, how many candles burn over all eight nights? | 36 | 8 | 44 | 64
exercise: Same Hanukkah, but now count the helper candle (shamash) lit every night as well. How many candles in all? | 44 | 36 | 45 | 52
exercise: Easter Day falls on 5 April. Pentecost is the 50th day, counting Easter as day 1. When is Pentecost? | 24 May | 25 May | 5 May | 26 May
exercise: Put in order: Pentecost, Good Friday, the Ascension, Easter Day. Which is second? | Easter Day | Good Friday | The Ascension | Pentecost
exercise: Which of these happened BEFORE Pentecost? | Jesus riding into Jerusalem on a donkey on Palm Sunday | Paul meeting Jesus in a light on the road to Damascus | Paul writing his letters to the churches in Rome and Corinth | The first churches growing in many cities around the sea
exercise: Ramadan began in March one year. Roughly when will it begin the next year? | About eleven days earlier, still in March or late February | About eleven days later, in early April | On exactly the same day, as it never moves | In the autumn, because it swaps seasons each year
exercise: Odd one out: Passover, Hanukkah, Yom Kippur, Eid al-Fitr. Which, and why? | Eid al-Fitr, because it is Muslim and the others are Jewish | Hanukkah, because it lasts for more than one night | Passover, because it is held in the springtime | Yom Kippur, because there is fasting on that day
exercise: Which pair are BOTH called festivals of lights? | Diwali and Hanukkah | Diwali and Passover | Eid and Pentecost | Wesak and Yom Kippur
exercise: Which sentence reports a belief fairly? | Muslims believe Jesus was a great prophet but not the Son of God | Muslims know nothing at all about Jesus | Muslims hate Jesus | Muslims and Christians believe exactly the same things about who Jesus was
exercise: Ash Wednesday is 18 February and Lent is "forty days". Why is Easter not on 30 March? | Sundays are not counted in the forty, so Easter comes later | Easter is always fixed on 1 April in every year | Lent is forty weeks, not forty days | The forty days start on Christmas Day
test: Bro in the group chat: "Moses was Joseph's dad, innit, they both did Egypt." What do you reply? | No, Joseph lived long before; Moses led the people out much later | Yeah, that's right, they were dad and son and lived at the very same time | No, Moses was Joseph's son, born in prison | No, neither of them ever went to Egypt
test: "Hanukkah's lowkey the Jewish Christmas, it's the same thing," says Kai. Best-reasoned reply? | No: it remembers the Temple made holy again | Yes, both festivals remember the birth of Jesus in Bethlehem | Yes, because both are always held on 25 December each year | No, Hanukkah is actually a Muslim festival that ends Ramadan
test: Gran: "I've told you a MILLION times, Pentecost is the day Jesus was born!" What is right? | Christmas is his birth; Pentecost is the Holy Spirit coming, after Easter | She's right, Pentecost is his birthday | Pentecost is the day of the Last Supper | Pentecost is the day he went to Egypt
test: Lily: "Jesus was at a Hanukkah thing? No way, that's literally impossible." Is she right? | No; John's Gospel puts him at the Feast of Dedication, Hanukkah | Yes, Hanukkah was only invented a long time after the year 1900 | Yes, the Gospels say Jesus never once went up to Jerusalem at all | No, Jesus started Hanukkah himself on the very day that he was born
test: A cartoon shows Noah's Ark sailing past the Tower of Babel with Moses waving from the deck. What's the slip? | Noah's flood comes before Babel, and Moses lives ages after both | Nothing, they all happened on the same day | Only that the Ark should be painted blue | Moses should be steering, not waving
test: "Easter's always the same date, mate, like Christmas." Is it? | No, Easter moves each year because it is fixed by the moon | Yes, it's always on 1 April | Yes, it's always 25 March | No, it's on any random day that the vicar of each church chooses
test: Dev: "Eid's fixin' to be in summer again, it's ALWAYS summer." What's the catch? | Eid moves about eleven days earlier each year, so over years it passes through every season | Eid really is always in summer | Eid is always on New Year's Day | Eid only happens once every eleven years
test: Maya says, "Muslims believe the Qur'an is God's word." Her friend says, "Say it's God's word, full stop, or you're being rude." What is fair? | Maya: she says what Muslims believe, kindly and truly | The friend: Maya is being rude by adding "Muslims believe" | Maya should call it just a made-up book to be really honest | Nobody should ever talk about other people's faiths at school
test: Sam's family have Shabbat from Friday sunset. Sam says, "So Shabbat's on Friday, done." What did he miss? | It ends at Saturday sunset | Nothing, Shabbat is only ever kept on a Friday | It's actually held on a Sunday morning instead | It lasts the whole week from one Friday to the next
test: Teacher: "Paul's road to Damascus came, er, before Jesus was born — no wait, after Pentecost." Which is true? | After Pentecost, as she said last | Before Jesus was born, as she said first | On Christmas Day, the day Jesus was born | On the very same day as Noah's flood
test: "Diwali is just for Hindus, nobody else bothers," says Leo. What is the fact? | Hindus, Sikhs and Jains all keep Diwali | Leo is right, only Hindus keep Diwali at all | Only Muslims keep Diwali, at the end of Ramadan | Nobody keeps Diwali any more, it stopped long ago
exam: Identify the sequence which correctly orders the following figures by their place in the biblical narrative. | Abraham, Jacob, Joseph, Moses | Moses, Abraham, Joseph, Jacob | Jacob, Abraham, Moses, Joseph | Joseph, Moses, Jacob, Abraham
exam: Ascertain the total number of candles lit during the eight nights of Hanukkah, the helper candle being lit on every night. | 44 | 36 | 40 | 72
exam: Select the explanation most accurate as to why Ramadan does not occur in a fixed season. | The Islamic calendar follows the moon and is about eleven days shorter than the solar year | The date of Ramadan is chosen anew each year by lot | Ramadan is fixed in the same week of every solar year | Ramadan is held only in years of a full harvest
exam: Which statement most accurately expresses the respectful reporting of a religious belief? | One which attributes the belief to its followers, as in "Christians believe", without mockery | One which declares the belief false | One which presents the belief as held by all people everywhere, without any exception at all | One which avoids naming the faith in question entirely
